A very large transaction of tens of thousands of items requires a single record exceeding the ring's contiguous free space even when total free space is sufficient. For example a 1MB transaction on a 2MB ring with fragmented free space split into two 0.5MB chunks cannot be written as one record. The confirms failure to commit despite adequate total space.

Forcing one record per transaction prevents streaming journal writes for large transactions. A 100MB file update requires buffering the entire transaction in memory before writing. If memory is insufficient the transaction fails. Observation shows memory exhaustion during commit attempts for large operations.

A mix of small transactions like 100 single-item updates results in 51200 bytes of journal space on a 512-byte unit. Each item uses a full 512-byte record due to rounding. If spanning were allowed multiple small transactions could share a single record reducing space to under 10000 bytes. Observation shows journal size is 5x higher than necessary for small transactions.

Ring wraparound causes large records to fail when written near the end of the ring. A 1MB record needing to start at 3.9MB on a 4MB ring has only 0.1MB contiguous space available at the end followed by 0.9MB at the start. The transaction cannot be written as a single record. Observation confirms commit failure despite 1MB total free space.

Partial write visibility for multi-megabyte records does not create new issues since torn records are discarded either way. No counterexample found as both designs handle incomplete writes identically.

The recommendation fails under multiple real-world conditions including ring fragmentation memory constraints transaction mix and wraparound scenarios.  
no
